WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.550
- By now, you've probably heard about

00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:02.900
the terrible acts of Ethan Crumbley

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.820
on November 30th, 2021

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:06.210
at Oxford High School.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.408
It's the latest in a long string

00:00:07.408 --> 00:00:09.010
of school shootings in America.

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:10.740
But what is legally interesting

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:11.770
about this case is that,

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:13.310
in addition to filing murder charges

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:14.252
against Ethan Crumbley,

00:00:14.252 --> 00:00:16.590
Oakland County Prosecutor Karen McDonald

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.080
decided to charge his parents,

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.280
James and Jennifer Crumbley,

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:20.880
with involuntary manslaughter.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:21.770
With this decision,

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:23.440
McDonald broke with other prosecutors

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:24.680
who have in the past declined

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:25.513
to charge parents

00:00:25.513 --> 00:00:26.760
for the acts of a child.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:27.593
So the question is

00:00:27.593 --> 00:00:28.570
what if anything is different

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:29.840
about this particular case

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.580
and does it signal a trend towards

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.080
making parents more responsible

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.173
for school shootings?

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.230
As is often the case these days,

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:40.550
many people are misunderstanding

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:41.560
the charges that were levied

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.240
against James and Jennifer Crumbley.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.050
Here, these charges hinge

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:46.500
on a difficult legal question.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:47.900
Could they have reasonably foreseen

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:48.780
that their teenage son

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.290
would use the gun they
purchased to kill people?

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:52.217
Did they willfully disregard

00:00:52.217 --> 00:00:53.770
the results to others

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:54.700
that might follow

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.600
from their action or failure to act?

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.860
And here, the prosecutor is arguing

00:00:58.860 --> 00:00:59.880
that the answer is yes.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.140
They knowingly armed a child

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.750
who had serious emotional problems.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:03.830
And when they were warned

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:05.420
that he was searching for ammunition

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:06.960
and had drawn a violent scene

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.102
involving someone being shot,

00:01:08.102 --> 00:01:09.880
they didn't search Ethan's backpack

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:11.040
or take him home.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:11.873
The defense lawyers

00:01:11.873 --> 00:01:13.330
will likely argue the answer is no.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:14.730
The parents locked up their gun,

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:15.990
consulted with the school officials,

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:17.443
and everyone reached the same conclusion

00:01:17.443 --> 00:01:19.300
that there was no risk that their son

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.160
would take a gun out and shoot people,

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:22.940
which is why he was sent back to class.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:23.847
Now, millions of parents,

00:01:23.847 --> 00:01:25.340
and by proxy their children,

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:26.680
are armed in America.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:28.100
And I'm sure the Crumbleys will argue

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:29.240
that they had no way to know

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:30.990
that this case would have been different

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.470
from all of the other millions of cases

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:33.310
that do not result

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:34.600
in intentional homicide.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:35.540
Here, Oakland County

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.180
filed involuntary manslaughter charges

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:38.930
against Jennifer and James Crumbley,

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:40.370
accusing them of failing to intervene

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:41.980
on the day that their
son killed four people,

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:44.130
despite being confronted
twice by the school

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:46.000
about their son's interest in weapons.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.620
According to the
prosecutor on Black Friday,

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.106
15-year-old Ethan went to his father James

00:01:49.106 --> 00:01:51.900
to purchase a nine millimeter SIG Sauer

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.270
from the Acme Shooting Goods.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:54.560
Ethan posted a photograph

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:55.880
of the new gun on his social media

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:56.713
later that day.

00:01:56.713 --> 00:01:57.970
The post said, "My new beauty,"

00:01:57.970 --> 00:01:59.050
with a heart emoji.

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:00.480
Jennifer Crumbley posted that the gun

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:01.860
was a Christmas present for Ethan.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.810
And they had enjoyed a mother-son day

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:04.643
at the range.

00:02:04.643 --> 00:02:06.365
On the following Monday, November 29th,

00:02:06.365 --> 00:02:07.720
one day before the shooting,

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:09.140
a teacher said that she saw

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:10.910
a boy searching online for ammunition,

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:12.774
which prompted a meeting
with school officials.

00:02:12.774 --> 00:02:15.130
The school left a voicemail
and sent an email,

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:17.000
but Jennifer did not
respond to the school.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.757
However, Mrs. Crumbley
texted her son, quote, "LOL!

00:02:19.757 --> 00:02:20.697
"I'm not mad at you.

00:02:20.697 --> 00:02:22.600
"You have to learn not to get caught."

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:23.857
On Tuesday morning, November 29th,

00:02:23.857 --> 00:02:25.458
the teacher found a
worksheet on Ethan's desk

00:02:25.458 --> 00:02:26.444
and took a photo.

00:02:26.444 --> 00:02:27.870
It was a drawing of a gun

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:29.067
pointing at the words,

00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:30.287
"The thoughts won't stop.

00:02:30.287 --> 00:02:31.120
"Help me."

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:32.150
There was also a drawing of a bullet

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:32.983
with words above it,

00:02:32.983 --> 00:02:34.010
"Blood everywhere."

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:35.080
There was a person between

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.040
the gun and the bullet.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:37.890
The person appears to
have been shot twice.

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:38.727
Ethan allegedly wrote,

00:02:38.727 --> 00:02:40.010
"My life is useless."

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:41.293
The teacher contacted the Dean of Students

00:02:41.293 --> 00:02:42.630
and the School Counselor.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:43.530
They called the Crumbleys

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:44.965
for an emergency meeting at the school.

00:02:44.965 --> 00:02:47.275
Meanwhile, Ethan was placed
in the counseling room

00:02:47.275 --> 00:02:48.500
with his backpack.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:49.740
The counselor obtained the drawing,

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.130
but Ethan had already
scratched out portions.

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:53.270
He allegedly said the drawing

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:54.340
was part of a video game

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.173
that he was designing,

00:02:55.173 --> 00:02:56.110
that he wanted a career

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:57.310
as a video game designer.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:58.300
Ethan stayed in the office

00:02:58.300 --> 00:02:59.550
for approximately 90 minutes

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.140
working on his science assignment.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.120
According to the Superintendent,

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:02.953
the counselor didn't believe

00:03:02.953 --> 00:03:03.786
that he would harm others

00:03:03.786 --> 00:03:05.780
based on his behavior or his demeanor.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:07.764
The Crumbleys arrived to
the school around 10:30 a.m.

00:03:07.764 --> 00:03:10.141
School officials showed
James and Jennifer the note.

00:03:10.141 --> 00:03:11.460
They asked Ethan whether

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.210
he wanted to harm himself or others.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:14.222
And based on his answers,

00:03:14.222 --> 00:03:15.640
the parents and school officials

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:17.212
assumed that he was not a threat.

00:03:17.212 --> 00:03:18.870
The Crumbleys resisted the idea

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:19.990
of their son leaving the school

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:21.500
and returning to an empty house.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:22.490
The school told the parents

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:24.690
to get Ethan into
counseling within 48 hours.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.404
According to Superintendent Tim Thorne,

00:03:26.404 --> 00:03:27.680
the school permitted Ethan

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:28.530
to return to class

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.460
because he had no disciplinary history.

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:31.860
At about 12:51 p.m.,

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.760
Ethan Crumbley emerged
from a school bathroom

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:34.610
with the gun his father

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:35.960
bought him four days before.

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:38.070
He fired at students in the hallway,

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:38.970
killing four,

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:41.140
and wounding six students and one teacher.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:42.267
After the shooting made the news,

00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:45.558
Jennifer texted Ethan
Crumbley at 1:22 p.m.,

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:47.110
quote, "Ethan, don't do it!"

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:48.130
Just 15 minutes later,

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:50.260
James calls 911 to report a gun

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:51.217
was missing from his house

00:03:51.217 --> 00:03:52.460
and he believed his son

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:53.300
might be the shooter.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:54.240
The Crumbleys said that the gun

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:55.620
had been kept in a locked drawer

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:56.730
in the parents' bedroom.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.130
So one of the things
that's interesting here

00:03:58.130 --> 00:03:59.485
is that the parents are not being charged

00:03:59.485 --> 00:04:01.980
by virtue of being parents,

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.880
but rather because of the actions

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:04.970
they took in general.

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:06.730
It's not a case of charging the parents

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:08.250
for what the child did.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:09.083
In other words,

00:04:09.083 --> 00:04:09.916
the argument is that

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:11.326
if anyone took the alleged actions

00:04:11.326 --> 00:04:12.840
that the Crumbleys took,

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.530
they could be charged with the same thing,

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:15.420
regardless of whether

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:16.660
they were the parents or not.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:18.150
Now to understand this distinction

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:20.070
and understand what is
happening in this case,

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:21.450
we need to talk about the different kinds

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:22.720
of homicide in Michigan.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:23.950
So first we start with murder.

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:25.190
First degree murder is defined

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:27.417
as a killing "perpetrated
by means of poison,

00:04:27.417 --> 00:04:28.250
"lying in wait,

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:29.717
"or any other willful, deliberate,

00:04:29.717 --> 00:04:31.140
"and premeditated killing."

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:32.510
Premeditated means the person

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:33.700
thought about committing the murder

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:34.737
before the act.

00:04:34.737 --> 00:04:37.240
This can take place weeks

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:38.240
or months ahead of time,

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:39.520
or it can take place

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:40.580
one second beforehand.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.606
For example, a Michigan
man named Kierelle Burns

00:04:42.606 --> 00:04:44.840
was convicted of first degree murder

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:45.673
after he made up a story

00:04:45.673 --> 00:04:47.350
about a robbery to lure his friend

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:48.210
into an alley

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:49.990
where he purposefully shot and killed him.

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:51.510
Burns said that he was acting

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:53.110
on the orders of a business associate,

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:54.320
but he still created a plan

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:55.450
for the murder ahead of time.

00:04:55.450 --> 00:04:57.830
And this is a classic
example of premeditation.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:58.940
And although the Burns case

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:00.170
is what most people think about

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:01.840
when they hear the words premeditation,

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:02.717
the term also applies

00:05:02.717 --> 00:05:04.860
when someone quickly makes up their mind

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:05.693
to kill someone,

00:05:05.693 --> 00:05:07.540
often in a split second.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.100
And here, Ethan Crumbley is charged

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:10.590
with four counts of first degree murder

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:12.050
and several counts of attempted murder.

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:13.350
So, no surprise there.

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:14.680
Then we get to manslaughter.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:17.485
In contrast, manslaughter
is murder without malice.

00:05:17.485 --> 00:05:19.830
Manslaughter offenses
are not premeditated.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:20.887
Manslaughter is generally defined

00:05:20.887 --> 00:05:22.920
as the crime of killing a human being

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:24.100
without malice of forethought,

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:25.450
or otherwise in circumstances

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:27.200
not accounting to murder.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:28.460
Michigan's criminal statutes

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:30.670
do not give a precise
definition of manslaughter.

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:32.560
As a result, Michigan courts look

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:33.740
to the common law

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:36.820
to define traditional
categories of manslaughter.

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:38.180
That is voluntary manslaughter

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:39.820
and involuntary manslaughter.

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:41.780
In Michigan, voluntary manslaughter

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:43.402
is often a heat of passion crime.

00:05:43.402 --> 00:05:45.230
It differs from first degree murder

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:46.258
because the intent to kill was formed

00:05:46.258 --> 00:05:48.710
when the defendant had a sudden violent

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:49.830
and irresistible passion

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:51.560
caused by some sort of provocation.

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:52.820
This provocation or excitement

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:54.030
has to happen almost immediately

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:54.863
without the chance

00:05:54.863 --> 00:05:56.040
for the person to cool down.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:57.300
And if the person had enough time

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:58.600
to get their emotions under control

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:00.251
and they still decide
to kill another person,

00:06:00.251 --> 00:06:02.730
then that is actual murder.

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:04.180
Now, involuntary manslaughter

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:06.510
occurs during an accidental

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:07.940
or non-intentional death

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:09.670
where the defendant had no intent

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:11.187
to cause someone else's death,

00:06:11.187 --> 00:06:12.710
but their reckless act

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:14.470
winds up resulting in death.

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:16.310
Involuntary manslaughter can also happen

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:17.560
during an unlawful act.

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:18.810
That is, a misdemeanor

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:20.800
or low-level felony like a DUI.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.000
Perhaps the most classic example

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:23.070
of involuntary manslaughter

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:24.050
is when someone is drunk

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:25.030
and gets behind the wheel,

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:26.630
and then kills another person

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:27.570
in a car accident.

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:28.560
Since everyone is presumed

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:29.890
to know that driving while under

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:31.390
the influence of alcohol or drugs

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:33.486
is reckless and puts others in danger,

00:06:33.486 --> 00:06:36.170
the resulting fatality is often charged

00:06:36.170 --> 00:06:38.090
as involuntary manslaughter.

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:39.147
Now, other reckless acts or omissions

00:06:39.147 --> 00:06:40.920
can lead to criminal charges too.

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:43.240
For example, if you decided to blow up

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:45.440
a giant firework in a crowded space,

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:46.497
and someone is hit and killed,

00:06:46.497 --> 00:06:49.880
that would probably be
involuntary manslaughter too.

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:51.970
Potentially something even more serious.

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:52.950
You might not have meant

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:54.450
to kill a certain person in the crowd,

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:56.186
but since it's so dangerous

00:06:56.186 --> 00:06:58.230
to engage in that activity,

00:06:58.230 --> 00:06:59.390
you would probably be charged

00:06:59.390 --> 00:07:00.770
with involuntary manslaughter.

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:01.710
Now under Michigan law,

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:03.550
to convict someone of
involuntary manslaughter,

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:04.610
a prosecutor must prove

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:05.496
beyond a reasonable doubt

00:07:05.496 --> 00:07:06.530
that the defendant

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:08.080
caused the death of the victim.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:09.280
That in doing the act

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:10.340
that caused the victim's death,

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:11.630
the defendant acted in

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:13.230
a grossly negligent manner.

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:14.440
And that the defendant caused the death

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:16.480
without lawful excuse or justification.

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:18.560
Now, proving someone
else's acts or omissions

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:20.080
actually caused someone else's death

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:20.980
is not an easy task,

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:21.860
especially when the people

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:22.900
who were charged

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:24.210
didn't commit the physical acts

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:25.600
that led to the death.

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:26.433
So in this case,

00:07:26.433 --> 00:07:27.280
that means the prosecution

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:28.980
has to prove that Ethan Crumbley's

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:30.600
intentional acts did not break

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.140
the chain of causation that started

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:34.410
when the parents purchased the gun.

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:36.266
The jury instruction
on causation explains,

00:07:36.266 --> 00:07:38.497
"It is not enough that the defendant's act

00:07:38.497 --> 00:07:40.637
"made it possible for the death to occur.

00:07:40.637 --> 00:07:41.877
"In order to find that the death

00:07:41.877 --> 00:07:43.317
"was caused by the defendant,

00:07:43.317 --> 00:07:44.907
"you must find beyond a reasonable doubt

00:07:44.907 --> 00:07:46.127
"that the death was the natural

00:07:46.127 --> 00:07:48.690
"and necessary result
of the defendant's act."

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:50.060
Now here, the prosecutor can show

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:51.770
that the son easily obtained the gun

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:53.720
as a direct result of
the parent's actions,

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:55.670
but that's not nearly enough.

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:56.550
The prosecutor will probably

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:57.670
also make the case that the Crumbleys

00:07:57.670 --> 00:07:58.830
failed to secure the weapon.

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:00.910
That the weapon wasn't locked up

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:01.743
or if it was,

00:08:01.743 --> 00:08:03.080
it wasn't properly secured.

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:05.870
And their son had easy access to the gun.

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:06.890
But in the Crumbley's defense,

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:08.150
their lawyers will probably argue

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:09.480
that the school didn't warn the parents

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:10.840
he had threatened to use a firearm.

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:12.120
That their complaint was only

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:13.310
that he had made a drawing

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:14.230
with violent imagery

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:15.180
including a firearm,

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:17.900
which probably millions of young boys

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:19.970
are doing every single day.

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:21.270
And while he was arguably

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:22.552
fantasizing about killing,

00:08:22.552 --> 00:08:23.810
the school and the parents

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:25.390
concluded that he wasn't a threat

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:26.660
to himself or others.

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:28.334
And Ethan's internet search for ammo

00:08:28.334 --> 00:08:30.200
was just that, a search.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:31.968
And of course, there
are many legal reasons

00:08:31.968 --> 00:08:34.080
to use ammunition in a gun.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:34.913
I'm sure they'll argue that

00:08:34.913 --> 00:08:36.630
given the short history
of the whole family

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:37.840
going to the shooting range,

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:39.210
that the most likely conclusion

00:08:39.210 --> 00:08:41.410
is that Ethan Crumbley was searching

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:43.440
for ammunition to use at the range

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:44.940
as they had done in the past.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:45.773
And on top of that,

00:08:45.773 --> 00:08:48.060
to prove the crime of
involuntary manslaughter,

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:49.890
the state has to prove that the Crumbleys

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:51.250
acted with gross negligence,

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:52.500
not simple negligence

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:53.716
where you act unreasonably.

00:08:53.716 --> 00:08:56.600
But gross negligence means
more than carelessness.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:58.120
It means willfully disregarding

00:08:58.120 --> 00:08:59.130
the results to others

00:08:59.130 --> 00:08:59.963
that might follow

00:08:59.963 --> 00:09:01.500
from an act or failure to act.

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:02.890
The prosecution must also prove

00:09:02.890 --> 00:09:03.766
that to a reasonable person,

00:09:03.766 --> 00:09:05.040
it would have been apparent

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:06.290
that the result was likely to be

00:09:06.290 --> 00:09:07.970
serious injury to another person.

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:10.368
And this is a very difficult case.

00:09:10.368 --> 00:09:11.860
Just because the Crumbleys

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:14.410
didn't act as another person might,

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:15.440
doesn't necessarily mean

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:17.350
that they acted with gross negligence.

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:18.670
And assuming that the Crumbleys

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:20.280
did not lock up their gun,

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:22.850
would that itself
constitute gross negligence?

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:23.860
Well, in many states,

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:26.380
it is a crime to not
properly store a firearm

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:27.640
when there is a child in the home.

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:29.080
But Michigan does not have any

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:30.410
Child Access Prevention Laws.

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:31.243
That doesn't mean it's,

00:09:31.243 --> 00:09:33.010
per se, unlawful for the Crumbleys

00:09:33.010 --> 00:09:34.740
to carelessly store their firearms.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:35.800
And of course, the Crumbleys

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:37.160
do not admit that they were negligent

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:38.830
in storing their gun and ammunition.

00:09:38.830 --> 00:09:40.365
And of course, not
searching Ethan Crumbley's

00:09:40.365 --> 00:09:42.246
backpack under these circumstances

00:09:42.246 --> 00:09:43.930
is also a point of contention.

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:45.140
I'm sure the prosecution will argue

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:46.559
that failing to search the backpack

00:09:46.559 --> 00:09:48.680
was also grossly negligent.

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:49.940
But under these circumstances,

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:51.670
the Crumbleys allegedly didn't find out

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:52.503
that their gun was missing

00:09:52.503 --> 00:09:54.020
until after the school shooting

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:54.853
had already started.

00:09:54.853 --> 00:09:55.910
And of course, the school didn't

00:09:55.910 --> 00:09:57.200
search the backpack either.

00:09:57.200 --> 00:09:58.140
Though there is one other issue

00:09:58.140 --> 00:09:59.430
that may hurt the Crumbleys's defense,

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:01.560
and that is their attempt to evade arrest.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:02.393
When there's evidence

00:10:02.393 --> 00:10:04.110
the defendant tried to run away

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:06.310
or escape after they
were accused of a crime,

00:10:06.310 --> 00:10:07.845
or police attempt to make an arrest,

00:10:07.845 --> 00:10:09.700
that can be used against the defendant

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:10.630
in court as proof that

00:10:10.630 --> 00:10:12.130
they had consciousness of guilt.

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:13.260
After the prosecutor announced

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:14.200
the parents would be charged,

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:16.140
the parents withdrew $4,000

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:17.910
and hid in a Detroit warehouse.

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:18.930
The Crumbley's lawyer said

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:20.260
that they were not fleeing from police

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:21.920
and they left town because
of violent threats.

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:22.840
Although their lawyer said

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:23.920
the couple was preparing

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:24.820
to turn themselves in,

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:26.210
the circumstances of their capture

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:27.043
don't look good.

00:10:27.043 --> 00:10:28.840
Now, while the circumstances of this case

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:30.590
are unusual and no doubt

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:31.977
will be difficult for prosecutors,

00:10:31.977 --> 00:10:33.480
there is some precedent

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:34.700
for charging parents

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:36.488
in somewhat similar circumstances.

00:10:36.488 --> 00:10:39.300
In 2020, Jamelle James pleaded no contest

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:40.357
to involuntary manslaughter

00:10:40.357 --> 00:10:42.240
after a six-year-old child in his home

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:43.898
shot and killed another
six-year-old child,

00:10:43.898 --> 00:10:45.820
Kayla Rolland, at school.

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:46.653
In that case,

00:10:46.653 --> 00:10:47.486
a witness demonstrated

00:10:47.486 --> 00:10:48.500
how they had seen James

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:49.947
and another man who stayed in the house

00:10:49.947 --> 00:10:51.010
playing with the gun,

00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:52.690
twirling it in their hands.

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:53.830
Now, other adults have been charged

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:54.960
with involuntary manslaughter

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:55.943
for not securing their guns,

00:10:55.943 --> 00:10:57.990
but prosecutors tend to charge adults

00:10:57.990 --> 00:10:59.960
only when the shooter
was someone very young,

00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:01.100
as in the Rolland case.

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:02.230
And that's because the law presumes

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:03.063
that children that young

00:11:03.063 --> 00:11:04.430
can't form criminal intent,

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:06.310
but the same isn't true of a teenager

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:08.189
who can form their own criminal intent.

00:11:08.189 --> 00:11:10.290
Teens are presumed to have agency,

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:12.190
which means that the chain of causation

00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:13.422
between the adults and the homicide

00:11:13.422 --> 00:11:15.070
is usually broken.

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:15.903
But there's no doubt that

00:11:15.903 --> 00:11:17.380
this is a tragic situation.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:18.530
And it's because of things like this

00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:19.470
that I think about trying to do

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:21.010
